@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Pages
@implements IHandleEvent
@using AwayWindow = SpaceSails.Client.Pages.Map.AwayWindow
@using NpcState = SpaceSails.Client.Pages.Map.NpcState
@using RouteWindowRow = SpaceSails.Client.Pages.Map.RouteWindowRow
@using ShuttleFarStop = SpaceSails.Client.Pages.Map.ShuttleFarStop
@using ShuttleStop = SpaceSails.Client.Pages.Map.ShuttleStop
@using System.Globalization
@using SpaceSails.Client.Rendering
@using SpaceSails.Contracts
@using SpaceSails.Core
@using SpaceSails.Core.Interior
@*
    #251 item 1 · THE SHUTTLE BAY CARD, ON ITS OWN FILE.

    Moved out of Map.razor by PURE MOTION — the block between the two fences below is the block that was in
    the page, character for character. Every page member it reads is a [Parameter] with the SAME NAME (the
    fields keep their underscore, the methods come across as delegates), which is what lets the markup move
    without a rewrite and lets the suite's source guards read it through MapMarkup exactly as before.

    The `@if` that opens it stays in Map.razor: the page keeps its paint order in one place, and a closed
    surface still renders nothing. IHandleEvent repeats the page's own override (Map.Sim.cs) — without it
    Blazor would put an automatic re-render back on every event in here, which the page turns off because
    the HUD's refresh belongs to OnTick's 200 ms throttle. The .razor.css beside this file carries no rules;
    it exists to give the component Map.razor.css's scope (#251 item 3 moves the rules).

    The `@{ }` around the fences is not decoration: the block used to sit inside `@if (…) { … }`,
    which is a Razor CODE block, and a code block is where its first statements are legal. The braces
    put the moved lines back in the context they were written in; they are outside the fences, so what
    the source guards read is still exactly the lines that were in the page.
*@
@* ── THE PAGE'S OWN NOTE ON THIS SURFACE, moved here with it (#251 item 1) and not touched by a
   word: it is the paragraph that stood above this block in Map.razor. It sits ABOVE the fence, not
   inside it, so that a source guard slicing the block by text goes on reading markup and not prose.
   ── *@
    @* The shuttle-bay airlock pop-up (#163, "the door you understand as a flight"): walking up to the
       bay's airlock lists the places within one shuttle hop. An interior berth is pickable — confirm and
       the crossing IS the trip, you step off ashore there; a body in range with nowhere to step off says
       so honestly; nothing in range keeps the door shut. Same overlay family as the deck-offer-card. *@

@{
@* ── MARKUP BEGINS · verbatim from Map.razor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        @* #997 wave 7 · THE LAST ROOT IN #735's CAP LIST TAKES THE SHELL, AND THE FOOT STAYS A ROW.
           #1007 named this one for wave 7: the only card in the capped-and-scrolling block still written
           without `::deep`, and the only one whose way out is a lone button inside a foot the page centres
           (`.deck-shuttle-actions`). A Bare shell's dismiss is the card's LAST DIRECT CHILD, which would
           have lifted "Close hatch" out of that foot and left it against the card's left margin — the exact
           shape that kept wave 6's logbook off the shell. So the shell learned the other half of #1007's
           own condition instead: WaysClass hands the page's foot the dismiss it draws. Same div, same
           class, same centring, same button — the shell wires it now, and its audit knows the ✕ has a verb.

           THE OTHER `.deck-shuttle-card` — the load-out (#313/#488) — is NOT migrated, and the reason is
           pinned by a guard rather than left in this sentence: see
           TheShellOwnsTheDeathRowsAndTheShuttleHatchTests. *@
        <OverlayShell class="deck-shuttle-card" Frame="OverlayFrame.Bare" Dismiss="OverlayDismiss.Close"
                      WaysClass="deck-shuttle-actions"
                      DismissClass="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light" DismissFace="Close hatch"
                      OnClose="() => Dismiss(CloseShuttleBayDoor)">
            <div class="deck-shuttle-title">🛸 Board the shuttle — where to?</div>
            @if (shuttleStops.Count == 0)
            {
                @* #368 (owner, 2026-07-19 playtest at The Red Eye's empty board): a fresh berth with nothing
                   in reach must explain itself in the house voice — apologetic and honest, then the nearest
                   ground below with a plain comparison to what the shuttle can actually cross. *@
                <div class="deck-shuttle-empty">
                    Sorry, captain — nothing within shuttle reach from this berth. The moons drift; watch the ranges below.
                </div>
            }
            else
            {
                <div class="deck-shuttle-rows">
                    @foreach (ShuttleStop stop in shuttleStops)
                    {
                        ShuttleStop local = stop;
                        <div class="deck-shuttle-row">
                            <div class="deck-shuttle-dest">
                                <span class="deck-shuttle-name">@(local.HasBerth ? "⚓" : local.IsLandable ? "🌑" : "•") @local.Body.Name</span>
                                <span class="deck-shuttle-meta">
                                    @FormatDistance(local.DistanceMeters) · ETA @FormatDuration(local.TravelSeconds)@(local.HasCache ? " · a chest waits here 🗺" : "")
                                </span>
                                @* #955 NAV-2: a row in reach is not a row that STAYS in reach. The window it
                                   is open for — and, on an armed plan, the RETURN BY it binds you to, because
                                   the ship flies on whether or not you are back aboard. *@
                                <span class="deck-shuttle-meta deck-shuttle-window">
                                    ⏳ @AwayWindowWord(WindowOn(local.Body.Id))@(RouteReturnBySimTime(local.Body.Id) is { } by
                                        ? $" · RETURN BY {RouteShuttleWindow.Stamp(by)} — the ship flies on"
                                        : "")
                                </span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="deck-shuttle-row-actions">
                                @if (local.HasBerth)
                                {
                                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-warning"
                                            title="Take the shuttle to @local.Body.Name — the clock advances by the crossing, then you step off ashore"
                                            @onclick="() => TakeShuttleTo(local)">Take the shuttle →</button>
                                }
                                @* #313: a landable surface — board it (destination, not intent). The tube grows in
                                   place; what you do on the ground is decided on the ground. *@
                                @if (local.IsLandable)
                                {
                                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-warning"
                                            title="Board the shuttle for @local.Body.Name — the down-tube grows on the map and you walk down. Bring cargo if you like; decide on the ground."
                                            @onclick="() => OpenBoardingPanel(local)">🛸 Board for @local.Body.Name</button>
                                }
                                @if (!local.HasBerth && !local.IsLandable)
                                {
                                    <span class="deck-shuttle-noberth"
                                          title="In shuttle range, but there's nowhere to step off yet">no berth ashore yet</span>
                                }
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    }
                </div>
            }
            @* #368 the honest tail: the nearest LANDABLE ground the shuttle can't reach yet — informational,
               never boardable (no buttons, visibly dimmed). Each row compares its current separation to the
               shuttle's reach and says whether the gap is closing or opening right now. When the board above
               is empty this IS the answer ("the nearest ones are:"); when it isn't, it's context underneath. *@
            @if (_shuttleBayFarStops.Count > 0)
            {
                <div class="deck-shuttle-farhead">
                    @(shuttleStops.Count == 0 ? "The nearest ones are:" : "Just beyond reach:")
                </div>
                <div class="deck-shuttle-rows deck-shuttle-far">
                    @foreach (ShuttleFarStop far in _shuttleBayFarStops)
                    {
                        ShuttleFarStop f = far;
                        <div class="deck-shuttle-row deck-shuttle-row-far"
                             title="In range of no shuttle from here — beyond the craft's @ShuttleReachText(f.RangeMeters) reach. Listed so you can watch the gap.">
                            <div class="deck-shuttle-dest">
                                <span class="deck-shuttle-name">🛬 @f.Body.Name@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(f.ParentName) ? "" : $" ({f.ParentName})")</span>
                                <span class="deck-shuttle-meta">
                                    @ShuttleSeparationText(f.DistanceMeters) · beyond shuttle reach (@ShuttleReachText(f.RangeMeters)) · @ShuttleTrendWord(f.Trend)
                                </span>
                                @* #955 NAV-2 · THE OWNER'S CORNER CASE, SAID OUT LOUD. Docked at a Jupiter or
                                   Saturn haven the moons swing in and out of shuttle reach every synodic
                                   period, so "beyond reach" is almost never permanent — it is a wait with a
                                   length. Where the geometry brings the ground back inside the horizon, this
                                   row says WHEN instead of leaving the captain to guess. *@
                                @if (AwayReopenSeconds(f.Body.Id) is { } reopensIn)
                                {
                                    <span class="deck-shuttle-meta deck-shuttle-window">
                                        ⏳ next window in @RouteShuttleWindow.In(reopensIn)
                                    </span>
                                }
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    }
                </div>
            }
            else if (shuttleStops.Count == 0)
            {
                <div class="deck-shuttle-empty">
                    No landable ground near enough to call either — the hatch stays shut for now.
                </div>
            }

            @* #955 NAV-2 · GO BY SHUTTLE, THE SHIP FLIES ON. Owner (2026-08-23): "with a pre-plotted, armed
               route you can LEAVE the ship by shuttle mid-voyage (meteorites, small sites) with a return-window
               clock … without a planned route you risk leaving the ship adrift." So this section exists ONLY
               while a plan is armed — the ship is then promised to fly herself (#969) and a RETURN BY is a
               promise the game can keep. Each row is one window the plotted path opens on ground ahead, read
               off the SAME samples the ARRIVE step reads; two rows for one moon are the owner's separate
               INGRESS and EGRESS windows. Informational: the boarding buttons above are still the only way
               aboard, and they light when a window is actually open. *@
            @{ var routeWindows = ShuttleRouteWindowRows(); }
            @if (routeWindows.Count > 0)
            {
                <div class="deck-shuttle-farhead">On the plotted route — go by shuttle, the ship flies on:</div>
                <div class="deck-shuttle-rows deck-shuttle-far">
                    @foreach (var win in routeWindows)
                    {
                        var r = win;
                        <div class="deck-shuttle-row deck-shuttle-row-far"
                             title="The plan carries her inside a shuttle hop of @r.Body.Name for a while. She will not stop and she will not wait: be off the ground by the RETURN BY or she goes on without you.">
                            <div class="deck-shuttle-dest">
                                <span class="deck-shuttle-name">🛸 @r.Body.Name — @r.Role window</span>
                                <span class="deck-shuttle-meta">@RouteWindowRowText(r)</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    }
                </div>
            }

        </OverlayShell>
@* ── MARKUP ENDS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
}

@code {
    // ── WHAT THESE PARAMETERS ARE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // #251 item 1 · Every [Parameter] below carries a member of the page under THE MEMBER'S OWN NAME —
    // a field keeps its underscore, a method arrives as a delegate with its own signature, a variable the
    // page's `@if` binds arrives under the name the `@if` gave it. That is the whole trick of this
    // refactor: it is what let the markup above move out of Map.razor without a single character of it
    // changing, and what lets the suite's source guards read it through MapMarkup exactly as they read it
    // when it lived in the page. A parameter that needs saying more than that says it on its own line.

    [Parameter] public List<ShuttleFarStop> _shuttleBayFarStops { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<string, double?> AwayReopenSeconds { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<AwayWindow, string> AwayWindowWord { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Action<NpcState> Board { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Action CloseShuttleBayDoor { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<Action, Task> Dismiss { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<double, string> FormatDistance { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<double, string> FormatDuration { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Action<ShuttleStop> OpenBoardingPanel { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<string, double?> RouteReturnBySimTime { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<RouteWindowRow, string> RouteWindowRowText { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<double, string> ShuttleReachText { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<List<RouteWindowRow>> ShuttleRouteWindowRows { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<double, string> ShuttleSeparationText { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<ShuttleExcursion.RangeTrend, string> ShuttleTrendWord { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Action<ShuttleStop> TakeShuttleTo { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<string, AwayWindow> WindowOn { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public List<ShuttleStop> shuttleStops { get; set; } = default!;

    // The page's own event dispatch, repeated: no automatic re-render per event.
    Task IHandleEvent.HandleEventAsync(EventCallbackWorkItem callback, object? arg) => callback.InvokeAsync(arg);
}
